      "answer": "Factor pairs: (1, 18), (2, 9), (3, 6)",
      "explanation": "Work systematically from 1 upwards until factors repeat."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex2",
      "card_color": "#1F5FA6",
      "card_bg": "#EEF3FF",
      "label": "Example 2 &ndash; using commutativity",
      "question": "Use the commutative law to write two multiplication facts from 7 &times; 8 = 56.",
      "steps": [
        "First fact: 7 &times; 8 = 56",
        "Swap: 8 &times; 7 = 56",
        "Both give the same product"
      ],
      "answer": "7 &times; 8 = 56 and 8 &times; 7 = 56",
      "explanation": "The commutative law means a &times; b = b &times; a always."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex3",
      "card_color": "#CC0000",
      "card_bg": "#FFF0F0",
      "label": "Example 3 &ndash; identifying missing factors",
      "question": "Find the missing number: ___ &times; 9 = 36.",
      "steps": [
        "We need a number that multiplies by 9 to give 36",
        "36 &divide; 9 = 4",
        "Check: 4 &times; 9 = 36 &#10003;"
      ],
      "answer": "The missing number is 4",
      "explanation": "Division is the inverse of multiplication; use it to find a missing factor."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex4",
      "card_color": "#1E7D2E",
      "card_bg": "#EDFAEE",
      "label": "Example 4 &ndash; factor pairs of a larger number",
      "question": "List all factor pairs of 36.",
      "steps": [
        "1 &times; 36, 2 &times; 18, 3 &times; 12, 4 &times; 9, 6 &times; 6",
        "Stop at 6 &times; 6 because 7 does not divide 36 exactly",
        "There are 5 factor pairs (including the square root pair)"
      ],
      "answer": "Factor pairs: (1, 36), (2, 18), (3, 12), (4, 9), (6, 6)",
      "explanation": "36 is a square number so one pair has the same factor repeated."
    }
  ],
